WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.119
Hi everyone it's a pleasure to be here thanks

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:14.149
[inaudible] for that introduction. I'm not a national hero,

00:00:14.149 --> 00:00:18.750
you have to play soccer and do it well to be national hero.

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:22.390
But it's really a pleasure to be here. As

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.419
[inaudible] was saying Mercado Libre basically started in

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:29.739
Stanford almost exactly eleven years ago; it was in March

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:35.399
and I was attending a class, a finance class taught by Jack

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:43.149
McDonald who is still teaching and I used my silver bullet,

00:00:43.169 --> 00:00:46.419
I don't know if you still have silver bullets or not. But I

00:00:46.419 --> 00:00:49.639
had used my silver bullet to attend his classes and he had,

00:00:49.659 --> 00:00:54.509
one day it was before Google and Google images. I always

00:00:54.529 --> 00:00:57.609
came to class and sat in the first row, I didn't study that

00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:00.359
much, well at Stanford but I really enjoyed coming to class

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:04.680
and to lectures and meeting the people that came to present.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.390
And one day I came in early and a guy was sitting there and

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:12.369
I didn't recognize him and he said 'hello I'm Joe Doe

00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:14.920
[inaudible] I'm just dressed up like Warren Buffet today. I

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.010
obviously didn't understand the joke so I said aha and I

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.329
said alone in a class wit Warren Buffet for five minutes; I

00:01:22.349 --> 00:01:25.349
didn't say a word to him. And the guy actually was Warren

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:29.769
Buffet so after that I decided that I had to use Jack

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:37.739
McDonald's contacts and I was preparing my business plan for

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:42.090
Mercado Libre which was an idea that I had, basically having

00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:46.929
lunch and dinners with friends here at school. And I asked

00:01:46.949 --> 00:01:50.099
him to introduce me to someone he thought might be

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:55.879
interested in investing in a project like mine. And he never

00:01:55.899 --> 00:01:59.120
said who is coming to class, partly to insure that everyone

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.819
comes to every class. And he said don't say this to anyone

00:02:04.839 --> 00:02:08.030
but John Muse was one of the founders of Hicks Muse, will be

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.069
coming to class in a month and I will tell him about your

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:17.259
project during lunch and I will set it up so you can drive

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:25.039
him to the airport after the class. So he did and before the

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:30.049
trip two of my classmates stepped into the car, they already

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:31.479
had like ten job offers,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.430
I didn't have anyone because I wasn't looking for a job; I

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.159
knew what I wanted to do.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.569
But they were the type friends that like to be near well

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.719
known people etc and they monopolized the conversation

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:47.879
during the trip and I was getting really nervous. We were

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:53.219
going down 101 and he had a private plane so it wasn't a

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:58.149
problem if he was there late. And so kind of half way

00:02:58.169 --> 00:03:01.829
through the trip he asked me so what are you going to do

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:05.949
after you graduate? And then I started with my pitch which I

00:03:05.969 --> 00:03:11.699
had rehearsed a hundred times but I knew I wouldn't have the

00:03:11.719 --> 00:03:15.539
time to deliver all of it, so I had to take the wrong exit

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.629
and took him to the commercial airport first, pretending

00:03:20.649 --> 00:03:23.370
that I didn't know he had a private plane. This was also

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.799
before 911, so after that we went to the private airport, in

00:03:28.819 --> 00:03:34.149
those days you went with the car to the plane. And he

00:03:34.169 --> 00:03:38.229
stepped of the car which was parked right next to the plane

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:43.529
and took his luggage and walked up into the plane and I was

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:46.299
standing there with my two friends and he left the luggage

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:50.049
and he walked all the way down and he told me next to my ear

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:54.109
'your story is fantastic and I'm fascinated by it and I want

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:55.129
to invest.

00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:00.139
Obviously my two friends didn't quite hear what happened and

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:02.909
they were extremely anxious that I was still so angry with

00:04:02.919 --> 00:04:07.309
them I refused to tell them. And I had already bought

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:11.269
tickets to go to Europe with my girlfriend after graduation,

00:04:11.289 --> 00:04:14.419
so I got home and I told my girlfriend 'I'm sorry but the

00:04:14.419 --> 00:04:18.519
trip has been cancelled' and we ended up going to Europe six

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:20.569
years later.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.729
But that's how Mercado Libre started and it's a true story

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:28.750
and it happened here in March of 1999. So with that, the

00:04:28.769 --> 00:04:33.139
idea today is to give you a brief overview of Mercado Libre

00:04:33.159 --> 00:04:39.959
and make this very interactive so feel free to interact with

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:44.379
questions as we go along. And afterwards if you don't, the

00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:47.180
presentation will be over in less than probably 20, 25

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:56.920
minutes maximum. And then we'll have time for Q &amp; A.

00:04:56.939 --> 00:04:59.689
So the origins of Mercado Libre as I mentioned; they started

00:04:59.689 --> 00:05:07.030
in June of 1999. This trip was in March of 1999, we started

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:11.459
the company in June and we launched in August as an online

00:05:11.479 --> 00:05:15.340
auction service in Argentina but we quickly expanded

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.769
regionally. We then did two rounds of financing. We did a

00:05:21.789 --> 00:05:27.050
first round of financing in October of '99 where John Muse,

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:32.500
Hicks muse invested, it was around 4.7 million dollars and

00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:35.629
they were joined by Chase Capital at the time.

00:05:35.649 --> 00:05:39.410
And then we did a second round of financing in May in 2000

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:43.810
for 46.5 million dollars which was led by Goldman Sachs. And

00:05:43.829 --> 00:05:49.300
obviously the first round investors also participated and we

00:05:49.319 --> 00:05:56.569
had a few other investors like GE Capital. So during 1999

00:05:56.589 --> 00:06:01.040
and 2000 basically we expanded regionally, hired a great

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.790
team, many classmates from Stanford, leading each one of our

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.649
different subsidiaries.

00:06:07.669 --> 00:06:13.680
In 2001 we partnered with Ebay. They had acquired a European

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:17.410
company that had a subsidiary in Brazil that was a

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:22.240
competitor of ours and in exchange for almost 20% of Mercado

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:26.689
Libre, they gave us that subsidiary and we signed our five

00:06:26.709 --> 00:06:32.879
year strategic partnership and non competition agreement,

00:06:32.899 --> 00:06:37.670
In 2002 we bought a Brazilian competitor called

00:06:37.689 --> 00:06:41.129
[inaudible], which was a strong local competitor in Brazil.

00:06:41.149 --> 00:06:44.209
In 2003, four years after the launch, we really started to

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:47.129
see some growth in the business. Before that it was all

00:06:47.149 --> 00:06:51.389
financing and marketing and focusing on product but not

00:06:51.409 --> 00:06:54.420
really that much traction in the business. So that's a big

00:06:54.439 --> 00:06:58.699
change from what you see today; companies like Groupon that

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:01.839
went from nothing to rumors say three billion dollars in

00:07:01.859 --> 00:07:05.199
revenue this year in two three years, it's a much bigger

00:07:05.219 --> 00:07:09.600
internet and many of the bottle necks that existed eleven

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:15.560
years ago have been solved, like payments for example.

00:07:15.579 --> 00:07:19.110
In 2004 we launched our payments platform and we learned a

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:27.379
specific business model for classified. In 2005 we broke

00:07:27.399 --> 00:07:29.649
even and we acquired

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:32.410
[inaudible] which was a regional competitor which was

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:36.860
launched right when we launched by a group of Harvard MBA's

00:07:36.879 --> 00:07:41.040
so that was a huge battle, that's also something I enjoy

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.319
telling Stanford grads.

00:07:43.339 --> 00:07:46.810
Specially when they ask me to call admins that have been

00:07:46.829 --> 00:07:51.370
added to both schools, I tell them the story; it works quite

00:07:51.389 --> 00:07:55.600
well. But it's a fascinating story because they were all

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.429
Harvard MBA's and we were all Stanford MBA's and we were

00:07:58.429 --> 00:08:02.560
competing in all of Latin America. And we built a great

00:08:02.579 --> 00:08:05.370
company and they got acquired by us in 2005 for 12 million

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:09.829
dollars we bought half of the operation and in 2008 we

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:13.810
bought the other half for 50 million. And today Medcado

00:08:13.829 --> 00:08:22.199
Libre is valued at almost 3 billion; so Standford is better

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:24.069
than Harvard.

00:08:24.089 --> 00:08:27.289
In 2007 we IPO'ed, we did a three hundred and thirty three

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:30.659
million dollar IPO which was a huge event because there

00:08:30.679 --> 00:08:39.029
weren't any tech IPO's those days and the day that we priced

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:44.159
was the first time since 911 that the European central bank,

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:47.370
the FED and the Japanese central bank, together had to

00:08:47.389 --> 00:08:51.750
intervene to provide liquidity to the markets because it was

00:08:51.769 --> 00:08:55.570
when the sub prime crisis was already at full speed and

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:59.769
there was a huge financial mess, which lasted obviously for

00:08:59.789 --> 00:09:05.059
several more months. We were quite unlucky, however the IPO

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:08.279
was very successful, unfortunately we priced at 18 that day

00:09:08.299 --> 00:09:12.740
and the stock closed at 28; so we left a lot of the money on

00:09:12.759 --> 00:09:16.500
the table there. And it's been going up since then.

00:09:16.519 --> 00:09:24.220
In 2008 we also acquired a classifieds player called tu

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.090
carro that is very strong in Venezuela and Columbia. And

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:33.970
more recently in 2009 we launched two new businesses called

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.210
Mercado Clics and Mercado shops. As you can see we're very

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:38.869
original with our names.

00:09:38.889 --> 00:09:48.409
Let me talk briefly about Latin America. It's a very

00:09:48.429 --> 00:09:51.730
interesting region, the one that we operate in. it has a

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:55.639
very good combination of actually GDP; population size and

00:09:55.659 --> 00:10:00.620
expected growth. We have almost six hundred million people

00:10:00.639 --> 00:10:03.889
in the region with a GDP per capita of close to nine

00:10:03.909 --> 00:10:08.750
thousand dollars and a total GDP close to five trillion

00:10:08.769 --> 00:10:10.929
dollars.

00:10:10.949 --> 00:10:14.909
In terms of internet we are seeing very strong growth but

00:10:14.929 --> 00:10:17.990
importantly we believe we will continue to see very strong

00:10:18.009 --> 00:10:42.039
growth going forward because in relative the internet

00:10:42.059 --> 00:10:45.700
penetration and broadband penetration is still quite low in

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.759
the region. But it's growing very fast. So we believe in the

00:10:48.759 --> 00:10:52.559
next ten years we will continue to see very strong growth in

00:10:52.579 --> 00:11:03.100
our business and in our industry.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.189
So this is the projection for e-commerce growth in relative

00:11:07.209 --> 00:11:14.019
terms. Latin America is still quite small but growing very

00:11:14.039 --> 00:11:18.610
fast relative to the other regions in the world and we

00:11:18.629 --> 00:11:21.199
believe this is going to continue being the case.

00:11:21.219 --> 00:11:27.340
Mercado Libre basically covers all of the region, it's most

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:31.449
important and largest markets and the objective is to cover

00:11:31.469 --> 00:11:35.439
all the region as our focus is mainly Latin America.

00:11:35.459 --> 00:11:43.939
We are the largest e-commerce site in the region and we are

00:11:43.959 --> 00:11:47.049
the largest e-commerce site in everyone of the countries

00:11:47.069 --> 00:11:52.309
that we operate as well. We are the eleventh largest retail

00:11:52.329 --> 00:11:56.100
site in the world; this is according to Comscore. The

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:01.779
largest is Ebay, second Amazon, in terms of unique visitors,

00:12:01.799 --> 00:12:05.669
very close to Ebay is Amazon, then Apple, Alibaba; that's a

00:12:05.689 --> 00:12:09.199
Chinese site and we are the eleventh largest in the world.

00:12:09.219 --> 00:12:12.490
In Latin America we are the first; we are three times larger

00:12:12.509 --> 00:12:17.429
than the second. Similar in Brazil, we're the first e-

00:12:17.449 --> 00:12:25.019
commerce site and Brazil is our largest market in the region

00:12:25.039 --> 00:12:28.970
as well and we're the leaders there and we're the leaders in

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.799
every single country where we operate as well.

00:12:31.819 --> 00:12:36.079
We're also one of the top internet destinations in the

00:12:36.099 --> 00:12:40.639
region. We're the eight largest destination in terms of

00:12:40.659 --> 00:12:45.949
unique visitors. So Microsoft is first, Google, Facebook and

00:12:45.969 --> 00:12:51.029
we're eighth and in Brazil we are the ninth; so we are also

00:12:51.049 --> 00:12:55.199
a popular destination there.

00:12:55.219 --> 00:12:59.549
The way we have implemented our business; we have four eco

00:12:59.569 --> 00:13:04.079
systems, what we're trying to create is a very powerful eco

00:13:04.099 --> 00:13:07.819
system made up of four smaller eco systems that feed each

00:13:07.839 --> 00:13:11.710
other. The largest businesses, our marketplace business and

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:16.649
I will review each one of these briefly. Then we have our

00:13:16.669 --> 00:13:19.600
payments business and our advertising and web stores

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:23.360
business.

00:13:23.379 --> 00:13:28.710
Our marketplace is basically the Ebay or Amazon type of

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:33.069
marketplace. It's interesting because we started being an

00:13:33.089 --> 00:13:37.319
auction site of used stuff and today ninety six percent of

00:13:37.339 --> 00:13:41.620
what is sold on our platform is fixed price and seventy five

00:13:41.639 --> 00:13:46.389
percent is new stuff. So we have evolved the model quite a

00:13:46.409 --> 00:13:51.779
bit; much closer to the Amazon marketplace. Having said that

00:13:51.799 --> 00:13:55.409
we're also very strong in C to C, combine the Amazon

00:13:55.429 --> 00:14:00.179
marketplace with craigslist, we're very strong in C to C as

00:14:00.199 --> 00:14:05.710
well. The 25% that is not new is used stuff sold by

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:09.399
individuals where we are also leaders there. And that is

00:14:09.419 --> 00:14:16.509
also a very attractive business to be in; the C to C.

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:20.199
We will the evolution of the figures so let me show you.

00:14:20.219 --> 00:14:24.319
This is prior to 2002; we were embarrassed to show the

00:14:24.339 --> 00:14:29.120
metrics but you can see very strong growth since last year,

00:14:29.139 --> 00:14:33.909
we recently announced out yearly results. Last year we had

00:14:33.929 --> 00:14:41.289
39 million transactions; that's an annual growth rate of 52%

00:14:41.309 --> 00:14:44.629
during the eight years. I think this is close to twenty

00:14:44.649 --> 00:14:50.039
times what we were in 2002. Last year we had fifteen million

00:14:50.059 --> 00:14:54.610
buyers and sellers actively using our marketplace and that's

00:14:54.629 --> 00:14:57.269
a figure that to me is very important.

00:14:57.289 --> 00:15:01.480
We had 4 million people selling on our platform and we had

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:05.990
52,000 people who make a living selling on our platform. So

00:15:06.009 --> 00:15:09.529
that's something that for me I'm very proud of. Typically

00:15:09.549 --> 00:15:12.929
when I go to one of our subsidiaries I go and visit several

00:15:12.949 --> 00:15:19.159
of our sellers and recently I've been very surprised by the

00:15:19.179 --> 00:15:21.959
size and the professionalism that these sellers have.

00:15:21.979 --> 00:15:26.159
Recently I was in Brazil with a seller who has 160

00:15:26.179 --> 00:15:32.039
employees. And they solely sell on our platform, so we are

00:15:32.059 --> 00:15:42.720
already enabling very large businesses. You have a question?

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:43.740
[Inaudible]

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:48.029
So the question is why is there such a big jump in paid

00:15:48.049 --> 00:15:52.309
listings in the last year?

00:15:52.329 --> 00:15:57.009
We have paid listings and we have free listings because more

00:15:57.029 --> 00:16:01.409
recently we introduced the ability for sellers to list

00:16:01.429 --> 00:16:06.759
something completely for free without any sort of fees and

00:16:06.779 --> 00:16:12.029
it has been a great move. It was risky at first because we

00:16:12.049 --> 00:16:16.269
didn't know how much it would cannibalize out own business

00:16:16.289 --> 00:16:20.490
and it turned out that we obviously grew listings a lot but

00:16:20.509 --> 00:16:23.509
we also grew revenues a lot because what we're doing is we'

00:16:23.509 --> 00:16:27.600
re convincing sellers to list for free initially, to try the

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:32.839
platform but then to upgrade to paid listings. So it has

00:16:32.859 --> 00:16:36.350
worked very well and we also improved our product quite a

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:40.289
bit in 2009 and 2010 and that enabled listing on our

00:16:40.309 --> 00:16:47.289
platform much easier than what we used to do in the past.

00:16:47.309 --> 00:16:50.809
In terms of gross merchandise volume; which is the value of

00:16:50.829 --> 00:16:55.970
the stuff sold on our platform; we grew from 55 million in

00:16:55.990 --> 00:17:03.149
2002 to 3.4 billion last year. This is like 60 X or 67%

00:17:03.169 --> 00:17:09.259
compounded annual growth rate. So those are some metrics

00:17:09.279 --> 00:17:10.279
that are interesting.

00:17:10.279 --> 00:17:14.230
Obviously the businesses continue to grow very fast and as I

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:19.099
was saying before, because of the characteristics of our

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:21.819
industry, will continue to grow very fast for many years and

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:25.000
that's one of the reasons we introduced free listings.

00:17:25.019 --> 00:17:29.839
Because there are millions of new users coming onto to the

00:17:29.859 --> 00:17:33.220
internet in Latin America this year. Which is not what you

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:36.149
find for example in more mature markets like the States

00:17:36.169 --> 00:17:39.009
where everyone has already been using the internet for a

00:17:39.029 --> 00:17:41.480
while. In Latin American; you have millions of people that

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:44.769
are accessing the internet for the first time. So we didn't

00:17:44.789 --> 00:17:48.139
want to have any barrier between these people and our

00:17:48.159 --> 00:17:51.230
platform. So we are happy to have them list for free and

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:55.690
then the free listings have less traffic so if we that those

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:58.930
listings don't convert, we email them and tell them hey why

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.869
don't you try upgrading to a listing that has more

00:18:00.869 --> 00:18:01.869
visibility?

00:18:01.869 --> 00:18:07.950
Our second business model, our second eco system is our

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:10.899
payments platform; that operates very much like Paypal. It

00:18:10.919 --> 00:18:16.269
used to be a closed platform, that it was only useful inside

00:18:16.289 --> 00:18:20.109
Mercado Libre and it had an escrow component and the buyers

00:18:20.129 --> 00:18:24.470
had to pay for the service because of the escrow service.

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:27.420
We've transformed that in our largest markets; Brazil,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:31.319
Mexico and Argentina, to a Paypal type of model where it's

00:18:31.339 --> 00:18:36.409
free for the buyers to use and it's growing very rapidly

00:18:36.429 --> 00:18:39.549
both inside out platform and outside of our platform. In the

00:18:39.569 --> 00:18:42.629
past, with the old version that we still have in our smaller

00:18:42.649 --> 00:18:48.349
markets, you could not use out payment system outside of our

00:18:48.369 --> 00:18:49.819
platform.

00:18:49.839 --> 00:18:52.740
So we believe this is going to be a business that has the

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:56.389
potential to be bigger and we believe this business will be

00:18:56.409 --> 00:18:59.379
bigger than Mercado Libre and we're seeing very rapid growth

00:18:59.399 --> 00:19:01.849
there.

00:19:01.869 --> 00:19:04.869
It's a very competitive business as well. We have local

00:19:04.889 --> 00:19:07.700
competitors and regional competitors here.

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:13.279
These are some metrics for Mercado Pago you can see a very

00:19:13.299 --> 00:19:18.359
big jump in the number of transactions processed and the

00:19:18.379 --> 00:19:21.500
total payment volume in the last two years as we transformed

00:19:21.519 --> 00:19:23.849
our business model and we improved our product.

00:19:23.869 --> 00:19:29.420
There's also a big jump in the adoption with the percentage

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:33.200
of listings within Mercado Libre that offer Mercado Pago. A

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:37.710
big change that we did is we used to be like Paypal, that's

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:43.279
optional for the sellers to accept Paypal or not; now in the

00:19:43.299 --> 00:19:46.639
countries where we have our new platform, it's compulsory to

00:19:46.659 --> 00:19:49.619
use Mercado Pago and it doesn't have a cost for the seller.

00:19:49.619 --> 00:19:53.960
The cost is imbedded in the Mercado Libre fee but every

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:57.059
seller has to offer Mercado Pago because we believe it's a

00:19:57.079 --> 00:19:59.779
much better experience for the buyer and our objective is to

00:19:59.779 --> 00:20:04.799
have 100 % of the transactions. We're at 20% currently or

00:20:04.819 --> 00:20:12.240
this was the average for 2010, Q4 was closer to 30%. Yes?

00:20:12.259 --> 00:20:13.259
[Inaudible]

00:20:13.259 --> 00:20:26.269
It's hard to test some of these things. What we typically do

00:20:26.289 --> 00:20:30.210
is we try it in a market and we see what happens, we

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:34.409
obviously look a lot at what's going on around the world and

00:20:34.429 --> 00:20:37.829
if you look at the most successful marketplaces which in my

00:20:37.849 --> 00:20:41.639
opinion is Itunes and the Amazon marketplace and more

00:20:41.659 --> 00:20:45.849
recently the Android marketplace. In those marketplaces when

00:20:45.869 --> 00:20:48.920
you buy you pay; there isn't a difference between buying and

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:51.490
paying. And in the Ebay marketplace and our marketplace

00:20:51.509 --> 00:20:54.579
still today; there is a difference between buying and

00:20:54.599 --> 00:20:57.349
paying. You buy and then you figure out with the seller how

00:20:57.349 --> 00:21:01.839
you wish to pay and that creates a lot of friction, a lot of

00:21:01.859 --> 00:21:04.809
hassle, it creates very high customer service cost for the

00:21:04.829 --> 00:21:10.159
seller and for us. So it was quite obvious to us that this

00:21:10.179 --> 00:21:13.500
is the way to go and we're going there but it creates a lot

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.250
of noise. When you have a big platform; four million people

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:20.720
selling on it and you create a big change like this, it

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:25.480
creates a lot of noise and you have to be able to maintain

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:28.420
your decision despite your noise. Ebay had a lot of those

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.440
problems some years ago when they started to do some

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:33.710
changes. I think Facebook is great at that, they make up

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:36.230
their minds about something and they do it and everybody

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:41.740
complains and they say, if they realize it's a mistake they

00:21:41.759 --> 00:21:45.059
go back. But if you have an eco system with millions of

00:21:45.079 --> 00:21:48.859
participants, you always hear lots of noise and you have to

00:21:48.879 --> 00:21:52.879
be careful to separate the noise between what is actually

00:21:52.899 --> 00:22:02.139
long term best for all the participants.

00:22:02.159 --> 00:22:05.619
So this is our payment platform and very briefly Mercado

00:22:05.639 --> 00:22:07.990
Clics and Mercado shops which are our two latest launches.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:14.720
Mercado Clics is basically an adwords type of service. In

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:20.000
the past it was almost heresy to let people go outside of

00:22:20.019 --> 00:22:23.250
our platform. So we didn't want anyone that was in Mercado

00:22:23.269 --> 00:22:26.490
Libre to leave our site.

00:22:26.509 --> 00:22:29.169
With the model we were very successful at attracting small

00:22:29.189 --> 00:22:33.109
and medium size sellers to our platform. But we were never

00:22:33.129 --> 00:22:37.460
really successful at attracting and retaining larger brands

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:41.839
to our platform, because they didn't want to list on our

00:22:41.859 --> 00:22:45.430
platform next to the small and medium sized businesses that

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:48.190
competed with them and they also wanted to control the

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:52.129
overall buying experience. But through Mercado Clics, we

00:22:52.149 --> 00:22:56.019
have been able to build very good relationships with very

00:22:56.039 --> 00:22:59.799
large retailers that are happy to be on our platform. And we

00:22:59.819 --> 00:23:02.379
also believe we are offering a better buying experience to

00:23:02.399 --> 00:23:06.009
the buyers who are now able to see in once place the small

00:23:06.029 --> 00:23:09.639
and medium size sellers and the abilities to also click away

00:23:09.659 --> 00:23:12.960
from there and buy in the large retailer if they want to.

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:18.279
Sometimes they prefer to buy in large retailers because of

00:23:18.299 --> 00:23:20.819
price, guarantees, brand recognition or special offers that

00:23:20.839 --> 00:23:24.899
they may have. So it has worked very well for us. We're

00:23:24.919 --> 00:23:29.359
obviously smaller than Google in the region but we are

00:23:29.379 --> 00:23:32.589
growing very rapidly and we have over 11,000 advertisers

00:23:32.609 --> 00:23:37.359
already, so this is a business that we believe has a huge

00:23:37.379 --> 00:23:41.279
potential as well. And finally we launched Mercado Shops

00:23:41.299 --> 00:23:46.379
which is a web front, web store solution and it's also

00:23:46.399 --> 00:23:49.069
growing extremely rapidly, we launched it in the second half

00:23:49.089 --> 00:23:54.799
of 2010 and we have already over 5000 web fronts, web stores

00:23:54.819 --> 00:24:01.159
active. And this is basically the ability for anyone to do

00:24:01.179 --> 00:24:04.259
e-commerce online and it comes integrated with Mercado Pago,

00:24:04.279 --> 00:24:08.909
Mercado Libre and Mercado Clics; so it's very easy to

00:24:08.929 --> 00:24:12.319
generate traffic to that store, if you have some inventory

00:24:12.339 --> 00:24:14.659
that you want to sell in Mercado Libre because you're not

00:24:14.659 --> 00:24:17.409
selling it in your platform; it's very easy to transfer the

00:24:17.409 --> 00:24:20.029
inventory to Mercado Lbre and sell it there and vice versa.

00:24:20.029 --> 00:24:25.710
And again it's a vision of having this full eco system where

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:28.609
each one of the parts helps the other.

00:24:28.629 --> 00:24:39.049
And finally some financials for Mercado Libre in 2005, we

00:24:39.069 --> 00:24:42.599
didn't dare go beyond 2005 so imagine what our revenues were

00:24:42.619 --> 00:24:45.899
our first five years of operations; not very substantial.

00:24:45.919 --> 00:24:50.299
But last year we had almost 220 million dollars of revenues

00:24:50.319 --> 00:24:55.000
and 56 million dollars of net income. And recently in our

00:24:55.019 --> 00:24:59.829
last earnings quarter we announced our first quarterly

00:24:59.849 --> 00:25:04.599
dividend and the policies to pay out 25% of the prior year's

00:25:04.619 --> 00:25:09.369
net income to the share holders. So it's a great feeling

00:25:09.389 --> 00:25:13.450
eleven years later to give back to out shareholders; some of

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:17.210
the money that they have invested. So that concludes the

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:20.349
presentation and we can now open it up to questions.

00:25:20.369 --> 00:25:28.779
When you created the company what were the major tweaks that

00:25:28.799 --> 00:25:32.019
you created compared to the Ebay model?

00:25:32.039 --> 00:25:36.829
Well we covered several of those but for the first few years

00:25:36.849 --> 00:25:41.049
we really tried to recreate Ebay but then we saw that

00:25:41.069 --> 00:25:45.349
several things didn't work. First of all people preferred

00:25:45.369 --> 00:25:50.250
fix price to auctions so we moved to auctions very rapidly.

00:25:50.269 --> 00:25:57.460
We've been at 90% plus fixed prices for the last eight, nine

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:01.440
years and Ebay is only at 60% fixed prices, going up because

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:06.240
they were three years ago at 40%. So I would say fixed

00:26:06.259 --> 00:26:10.339
prices is a big one. The recent changes that we have done

00:26:10.359 --> 00:26:13.789
with the payments; making Mercado Pago compulsory to other

00:26:13.809 --> 00:26:16.990
sellers and we're moving forward to make the usage

00:26:17.009 --> 00:26:19.379
compulsory, also to buyers I think it's going to be a big

00:26:19.379 --> 00:26:26.069
difference. The way that we approach classifieds, we quickly

00:26:26.089 --> 00:26:30.220
realized that people were not buying cats in auctions even

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:35.309
though they might or they were doing it in the US, we didn't

00:26:35.329 --> 00:26:39.869
think that was the right model so we very rapidly did a

00:26:39.889 --> 00:26:43.429
completely different business model for classifieds with

00:26:43.449 --> 00:26:47.440
different pricing, different navigation, different search

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:50.909
and it worked very well. We are the leaders in motors in

00:26:50.929 --> 00:26:57.579
every country and that's not the case for Ebay in the US.

00:26:57.599 --> 00:27:07.569
There are very strong vehicle sites here in the US. So I

00:27:07.589 --> 00:27:13.169
think those probably are the biggest tweaks. Sorry, finally

00:27:13.189 --> 00:27:16.929
the free listings and the way that we have been able to

00:27:16.949 --> 00:27:20.009
remain very strong and I would say leaders in classifieds.

00:27:20.029 --> 00:27:25.259
Here you have craigslist which is very big and very strong.

00:27:25.279 --> 00:27:28.750
You don't have anything, nowhere near to craigslist in Latin

00:27:28.769 --> 00:27:32.099
America, even though free classifieds have existed there for

00:27:32.119 --> 00:27:36.799
many years and I believe the reason is that we captured the

00:27:36.819 --> 00:27:43.259
C to C segment better than what Ebay here. And more recently

00:27:43.279 --> 00:27:48.069
allowing free classifieds, free listings has been a good

00:27:48.089 --> 00:27:49.089
part of that.

00:27:49.089 --> 00:27:51.839
Meg Whitman was actually here on Tuesday and she called a

00:27:51.839 --> 00:27:57.950
decision to join venture with you as opposed to try and go

00:27:57.970 --> 00:28:01.220
into the market; one of her best decisions as CEO of Ebay. I

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:04.740
was wondering from your perspective, what were the pros and

00:28:04.759 --> 00:28:09.039
cons of receiving a secret investment from Ebay than having

00:28:09.059 --> 00:28:10.599
them as a fairly significant shareholder?

00:28:10.619 --> 00:28:15.409
Yeah I was with Meg last night and she told me she was here

00:28:15.429 --> 00:28:24.440
a few days ago. So it was, for us it was a very big win to

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:29.230
partner with Ebay. When we signed, we knew it was going to

00:28:29.250 --> 00:28:33.339
be good, we weren't sure why but we were willing to do it

00:28:33.359 --> 00:28:36.539
almost at any price and we were lucky that Ebay didn't want

00:28:36.539 --> 00:28:40.549
to consolidate our losses into their PNL. They wanted to be

00:28:40.569 --> 00:28:48.109
under 20% of ownership. But we were willing to do it, not ay

00:28:48.129 --> 00:28:51.419
any price, but we really wanted to a do a deal, we didn't

00:28:51.419 --> 00:28:55.169
know exactly why, when we did it. We signed a best practice

00:28:55.189 --> 00:28:59.519
sharing agreement and a non compete agreement. And the best

00:28:59.539 --> 00:29:02.409
practice sharing which we didn't value that much at the time

00:29:02.409 --> 00:29:06.529
ended up being extremely valuable. Because coming to the

00:29:06.549 --> 00:29:08.799
quarterly meetings with Ebay and sharing knowledge with

00:29:08.819 --> 00:29:13.109
them, learning from the things they were doing right and the

00:29:13.129 --> 00:29:16.559
things that they were doing right was very advantageous for

00:29:16.579 --> 00:29:22.049
us and I think it was also very helpful for them because we

00:29:22.069 --> 00:29:25.019
didn't need their approval to do anything so we would go and

00:29:25.019 --> 00:29:28.889
do things radically different to them and they would learn

00:29:28.909 --> 00:29:31.139
from that as well. So I think it was mutually convenient.

00:29:31.139 --> 00:29:32.139
Obviously the

00:29:32.139 --> 00:29:39.200
[inaudible] o would prefer to not to get diluted 20% but

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:40.480
there's a price to everything.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:41.480
[Inaudible]

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:57.500
Obviously the partnership has evolved a lot. When we signed

00:29:57.519 --> 00:30:00.429
it we were almost like a subsidiary of Ebay. And the non

00:30:00.429 --> 00:30:03.829
competing competition agreement expired five years ago, it

00:30:03.849 --> 00:30:08.339
lasted five years 2001 to 2006. And now we compete, we

00:30:08.359 --> 00:30:11.740
compete with their classified, we compete with Paypal. We

00:30:11.759 --> 00:30:14.629
have a very good relationship with them; they're our largest

00:30:14.649 --> 00:30:19.279
minority shareholder but they don't have a board seat. And

00:30:19.299 --> 00:30:21.649
we have a very good relationship but we are also

00:30:21.649 --> 00:30:26.470
competitors. Which is not unusual in our industry, we are

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:29.539
partners with Google, we are also competitors with Google

00:30:29.559 --> 00:30:34.159
and Facebook etc.

00:30:34.179 --> 00:30:37.339
You touched briefly on the tension between C to C and B to

00:30:37.339 --> 00:30:46.769
C, can you talk some more on your strategy of how to keep

00:30:46.789 --> 00:30:48.789
the marketplace open for C to C as

00:30:48.789 --> 00:30:49.789
[inaudible]

00:30:49.789 --> 00:30:51.789
Yeah I don't see those two things as conflicting even though

00:30:51.809 --> 00:30:55.720
intuitively they look very conflicting; you say I have this

00:30:55.740 --> 00:31:00.019
used mouse to see, how will anybody see that mouse if there

00:31:00.039 --> 00:31:05.419
are 5000 mice being sold by Best Buy or other large

00:31:05.439 --> 00:31:11.549
retailers? It's interesting but it gets sold anyhow. If you

00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:15.169
have the right place. Obviously C to C have a way for them

00:31:15.189 --> 00:31:18.250
to gain market share within our marketplace is to lower

00:31:18.269 --> 00:31:23.049
prices. But the way the internet works, people don't

00:31:23.069 --> 00:31:26.399
necessarily do a search and go page by page until they find

00:31:26.419 --> 00:31:31.289
inventory. They access inventory through natural search in

00:31:31.309 --> 00:31:36.409
Google. I don't know how they do it but they get to the

00:31:36.429 --> 00:31:43.339
inventory and the 75/25 % mix has been relatively stable

00:31:43.359 --> 00:31:46.569
across the different countries. So there is good liquidity

00:31:46.589 --> 00:31:51.139
in the C to C marketplace as well even though free listings

00:31:51.159 --> 00:31:55.779
are the ones that you see last as a buyer when you do a

00:31:55.799 --> 00:31:56.799
search.

00:31:56.799 --> 00:32:08.579
Question: I understand that the model is shifting a little

00:32:08.599 --> 00:32:11.349
towards the Amazon business model. If this is true how are

00:32:11.349 --> 00:32:17.069
you going to, are you going to warehouses to work more on

00:32:17.089 --> 00:32:18.339
the logistics of the business?

00:32:18.339 --> 00:32:22.529
That's a great question. We are not into logistics today and

00:32:22.549 --> 00:32:26.389
we do not have plans to get into logistics ourselves, yet.

00:32:26.409 --> 00:32:29.559
But that's something that we are looking at closely and we'

00:32:29.579 --> 00:32:34.939
ll see what we need to do as the market evolves. But

00:32:34.959 --> 00:32:37.990
currently what we are going to be doing is partnering with

00:32:38.009 --> 00:32:41.500
the people that do logistics in the region and having them

00:32:41.519 --> 00:32:45.980
do logistics for us but we plan to get involved much more

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:48.879
significantly in the shipping process than what we are

00:32:48.899 --> 00:32:53.710
today. Today we're letting the seller solve shipping, each

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:55.679
seller might have a different solution; there's an

00:32:55.679 --> 00:33:00.389
opportunity to get in the middle and negotiate better prices

00:33:00.409 --> 00:33:03.769
on behalf of all of sellers and whenever there's a problem

00:33:03.789 --> 00:33:07.269
have ourselves interacting with the logistics provider

00:33:07.289 --> 00:33:09.990
rather than the different sellers. So there is an

00:33:10.009 --> 00:33:12.969
opportunity to improve what we are doing and that's what we

00:33:12.969 --> 00:33:16.169
are going to be working on in the next two years.

00:33:16.189 --> 00:33:19.759
Basically with e-commerce there are two very large friction

00:33:19.779 --> 00:33:23.399
payments. One is payments and the other one is shipping. We

00:33:23.419 --> 00:33:27.079
believe payments; we are very close to solving it with these

00:33:27.099 --> 00:33:31.689
things I was telling you were are doing with Mercado Pago.

00:33:31.709 --> 00:33:37.409
And shipping is the next big thing for us to solve.

00:33:37.429 --> 00:33:41.730
And we intend to solve it using a similar approach to the

00:33:41.750 --> 00:33:44.029
one we have been using with payments.

00:33:44.049 --> 00:34:05.519
Do you believe the Amazon model is feasible in Latin

00:34:05.539 --> 00:34:06.789
America? Basically because sometimes infrastructure,

00:34:06.789 --> 00:34:09.539
especially postal services tend not to be as solid and if

00:34:09.539 --> 00:34:12.539
you were to apply it would you partner with people that are

00:34:12.539 --> 00:34:14.789
already doing logistics? I've seen that Mercado Libre is

00:34:14.789 --> 00:34:17.289
tweaking ever model and bringing it to Latin America, what

00:34:17.289 --> 00:34:19.539
would be the necessary tweaks to make it work?

00:34:19.539 --> 00:34:21.789
Well logistics in Latin America has many issues particularly

00:34:21.789 --> 00:34:24.789
unions. So we are happy not to logistics and I think it's

00:34:24.789 --> 00:34:27.539
going to be very difficult to get into logistics in Latin

00:34:27.539 --> 00:34:30.039
America when you add the unions factor. Logistics unions are

00:34:30.039 --> 00:34:32.789
very powerful in some of the countries. So we initially are

00:34:32.789 --> 00:34:35.289
not intending to do it ourselves. Whether or not it's

00:34:35.289 --> 00:34:37.559
feasible, I don't know. It's a question for someone like

00:34:37.559 --> 00:34:38.559
Amazon to solve.

00:34:38.559 --> 00:34:41.139
Question: Many of us are thinking of starting internet

00:34:41.159 --> 00:34:43.409
companies after graduation. What are some of the common

00:34:43.409 --> 00:34:50.889
mistakes that you would think we will make in our first year

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:51.909
of being in business?

00:34:51.909 --> 00:34:57.719
Well I can tell you the ones we made. We thought we were

00:34:57.739 --> 00:35:04.059
going to grow much faster than what we did. And it was

00:35:04.079 --> 00:35:07.949
probably a consequence of the days when we launched, as I

00:35:07.969 --> 00:35:11.539
said before, today things have changed dramatically, you see

00:35:11.559 --> 00:35:14.230
companies like Groupon and

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:17.539
[inaudible] really scaling very fast because the internet is

00:35:17.559 --> 00:35:21.289
much bigger and many of the bottle necks like payments have

00:35:21.309 --> 00:35:25.440
been solved for them already. But we thought we were going

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:26.460
to scale much faster.

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:31.699
One thing I would do if I were to start again is whenever

00:35:31.719 --> 00:35:37.840
you do a round of financing, secure more funding than you

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:41.179
think you might need, in case you don't grow as fast or you

00:35:41.179 --> 00:35:46.119
have to spend more that what you thought. They be very

00:35:46.139 --> 00:35:49.489
careful as to how you spend the money. I think one things we

00:35:49.509 --> 00:35:52.400
did right, relative to everyone else in the region is we

00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:57.219
really saved the money for the rainy days and then when the

00:35:57.239 --> 00:36:00.329
bubble burst and no one was able to access capital any

00:36:00.349 --> 00:36:05.670
longer and the business wasn't there because, when we

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:09.900
launched internet penetration, the region was 3%, today it's

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:15.279
close to 40%. So E-commerce which was 10% of the 3% is

00:36:15.299 --> 00:36:18.739
really very small, so all o four competitors ran out of cash

00:36:18.759 --> 00:36:24.009
and we still had 40 million dollars in the bank. So that was

00:36:24.029 --> 00:36:28.250
important and I think the key, the most important thing for

00:36:28.269 --> 00:36:31.559
technology start ups is to focus on the product and get the

00:36:31.579 --> 00:36:35.440
product right and if there is something I regret it's not

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.840
having a stronger technology background, it's something that

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:43.809
I think Stanford should provide the GSB. You graduate here

00:36:43.829 --> 00:36:48.549
knowing accounting and you can interact with an auditor and

00:36:48.569 --> 00:36:53.949
discuss a PNL with them but you cannot interact with a

00:36:53.969 --> 00:36:57.860
developer and discuss the way they have written code so

00:36:57.880 --> 00:37:01.110
that's a big handicap for technology start ups. So I you

00:37:01.130 --> 00:37:03.630
don't have that background like I did, I think it's

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:06.130
important to find a partner that has a very strong

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:08.529
technology background. Because that's the most important

00:37:08.549 --> 00:37:09.549
thing; the product.

00:37:09.549 --> 00:37:20.139
When you decided to start your company how did you go about

00:37:20.159 --> 00:37:21.409
the decision process of incorporating

00:37:21.409 --> 00:37:27.110
[inaudible] as opposed to going back to Argentina

00:37:27.130 --> 00:37:28.130
[inaudible]

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:41.339
Well it's a way you want to live your life. There's no right

00:37:41.359 --> 00:37:44.960
or wrong there. I was with my girlfriend, we wanted to go

00:37:44.980 --> 00:37:48.980
back to Argentina so that's why we decided to go back. But

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:53.219
obviously it's an amazing place to live and do business in.

00:37:53.239 --> 00:37:55.549
it's obviously more competitive as well, much more

00:37:55.569 --> 00:37:59.980
competitive.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:13.880
How are you overcoming the volume from Latin American

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:16.150
consumers to train online or to give their personal

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:17.400
information to an online platform.

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.650
[inaudible] tons of data from your consumers, what are you

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:23.389
as a company actually doing to limit that? And

00:38:23.409 --> 00:38:24.409
[inaudible]

00:38:24.409 --> 00:38:29.469
So the barriers, it's time and it's product. If you have a

00:38:29.489 --> 00:38:33.880
good product people buy online and they don't see any virus.

00:38:33.900 --> 00:38:38.299
Now with the new payments platform, the second time you buy

00:38:38.319 --> 00:38:41.829
online, you do three clicks and your credit card is stored

00:38:41.849 --> 00:38:44.819
there, your shipping address is stored there and it's really

00:38:44.839 --> 00:38:48.139
easy, you see buyer's protection and people feel very safe

00:38:48.159 --> 00:38:52.299
doing it and we have a very high repeat ratio of buyers that

00:38:52.319 --> 00:38:56.799
come and buy again. So having a good product is helpful and

00:38:56.819 --> 00:38:59.969
then it's time. Some people need some time, they hear from

00:38:59.989 --> 00:39:07.949
their friends, I bought this on Mercado Libre, it's really

00:39:07.969 --> 00:39:10.569
easy, it's very simple. An internet penetration increases

00:39:10.589 --> 00:39:17.589
those barriers come down. And in respect to the data, I

00:39:17.609 --> 00:39:22.299
think we could do a much better job using our data. We don't

00:39:22.319 --> 00:39:25.440
intend to monetize the data, selling it to third parties or

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:30.489
things like that but one of the big things we are starting

00:39:30.509 --> 00:39:34.429
to look at is doing the famous 'people who have seen this

00:39:34.449 --> 00:39:37.380
have also bought that and people who have bought this has

00:39:37.400 --> 00:39:41.029
also bought that' and we're just in the very early days of

00:39:41.049 --> 00:39:46.409
that but to do that well you need to catalogue, structure

00:39:46.429 --> 00:39:52.339
all the data. And our data was unstructured, our marketplace

00:39:52.359 --> 00:39:56.789
data. So we've done a very big effort in building our own

00:39:56.809 --> 00:40:00.440
catalogue of products trying to structure that data so we

00:40:00.460 --> 00:40:04.739
know that people that bought exactly this mouse bought

00:40:04.759 --> 00:40:08.230
exactly this monitor and not just any other monitor. So once

00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:11.289
you have the catalogue, it's much easier to do those

00:40:11.309 --> 00:40:12.309
recommendations.

00:40:12.309 --> 00:40:17.940
What are the things that you did different from other

00:40:17.960 --> 00:40:19.210
markets that made a difference?

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:28.000
As I said before, I think we focused much more on product;

00:40:28.019 --> 00:40:34.029
less on PR and marketing, they are very good at that, we're

00:40:34.049 --> 00:40:38.569
not good at that so we focused on product and we saved our

00:40:38.589 --> 00:40:46.529
money and they spent it all. I think overall we had a long

00:40:46.549 --> 00:40:50.889
term vision of building a company. And that's what I would

00:40:50.909 --> 00:40:55.690
encourage anyone's who is thinking of doing a startup is to

00:40:55.710 --> 00:40:59.099
have a long term vision of building a company, because you

00:40:59.119 --> 00:41:03.110
have many difficulties along the way and if your motivation

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:07.989
is just to do an IPO or have an exit or sell the company,

00:41:08.009 --> 00:41:11.170
it's not enough. Sometimes you need a lot of patience like

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:14.639
we needed and we had a long term vision of building a

00:41:14.659 --> 00:41:18.719
company that added value to millions of people. I think that

00:41:18.739 --> 00:41:19.739
was very powerful

00:41:19.739 --> 00:41:20.739
[Inaudible]

00:41:20.739 --> 00:41:30.099
Well we have a huge challenge because the technology

00:41:30.119 --> 00:41:34.880
industry is incredibly competitive. So whatever you did

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:39.500
right in the past is probably a weakness today because you

00:41:39.519 --> 00:41:43.829
have lots of legacy and we have lots of legacy. If you look

00:41:43.849 --> 00:41:47.779
at the players in the US, there are very few internet

00:41:47.799 --> 00:41:50.480
companies that started in 1999 when we started, that are

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:54.869
successful today and that's because technology wise it was

00:41:54.889 --> 00:41:58.199
hard for them to adapt and we're in the process of adapting.

00:41:58.219 --> 00:42:02.849
We have re-written all of our code to have an open platform

00:42:02.869 --> 00:42:08.110
with API's and to enable third parties to develop on top of

00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:11.299
our platform. That's something we didn't have, it's still

00:42:11.319 --> 00:42:16.360
not live today and that's going to be a major change. So

00:42:16.380 --> 00:42:20.299
basically transforming our technology is a big challenge and

00:42:20.319 --> 00:42:25.449
if we do that well I think we have tremendous potential

00:42:25.469 --> 00:42:28.880
because I believe the growth in next years is going to be

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:31.150
much faster than the growth in the last years.

00:42:31.159 --> 00:42:37.289
So with Mercado shops, you've kind of gone away from your

00:42:37.309 --> 00:42:41.299
own destination site to more of a distributed model. What

00:42:41.319 --> 00:42:45.339
are your thoughts of that in the future? Is it going to

00:42:45.359 --> 00:42:48.359
consolidate more or is it going to go more in the direction

00:42:48.359 --> 00:42:51.819
of commerce everywhere out there? Where is it going to go?

00:42:51.839 --> 00:42:54.589
It's going to go in the latter. Commerce everywhere. So if

00:42:54.589 --> 00:42:57.429
you look at it Mercado Shops, Mercado Clics and Mercado

00:42:57.449 --> 00:42:58.449
Pago, the

00:42:58.449 --> 00:43:03.440
[inaudible] is also that so while Mercado Libre continues to

00:43:03.460 --> 00:43:08.699
grow very rapidly and is our largest business, we believe

00:43:08.719 --> 00:43:12.599
it's going to go towards and open model and that's why we

00:43:12.619 --> 00:43:17.110
launched Mercado Shops. Mercado shops acts if you will as a

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:20.880
distributor of our payments platform and Mercado Clics, our

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:23.239
traffic generation platform. And also as Mercado Libre

00:43:23.259 --> 00:43:26.889
because you can very easily transfer inventory from one to

00:43:26.909 --> 00:43:31.049
the other. So Mercado Libre is becoming more and more of an

00:43:31.069 --> 00:43:37.319
open platform as well. And with API's etc, the vision is to

00:43:37.339 --> 00:43:40.940
have, let's say you list an item on our platform, it's like

00:43:40.960 --> 00:43:46.099
listing a video on Youtube, then that item can appear as a

00:43:46.119 --> 00:43:48.869
video on Youtube, anywhere, in any blog , you can see that

00:43:48.869 --> 00:43:53.429
item anywhere and buy it. So that's a vision that we have

00:43:53.449 --> 00:43:56.699
for Mercado Libre and that's the part of transforming our

00:43:56.719 --> 00:44:01.380
technology that we're doing. Rather than going to Mercado

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:05.420
Libre to see this item or video you can see anywhere, you

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:09.039
can embed that item anywhere and even buy it anywhere; from

00:44:09.059 --> 00:44:23.529
a mobile phone, from a tablet etc.

00:44:23.549 --> 00:44:44.480
[Inaudible] what are you doing to raise those very raise

00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:49.119
those very difficult barriers to allow commerce to flow?

00:44:49.139 --> 00:44:53.469
There's very little cross border trade today. Payment is a

00:44:53.489 --> 00:44:57.150
big part of solving that. But it's highly regulated and the

00:44:57.170 --> 00:45:01.699
people doing it are not public companies that need to comply

00:45:01.719 --> 00:45:08.039
with all the laws that we do. It's very complex to transfer

00:45:08.059 --> 00:45:11.039
money from Argentina to Peru or from Argentina to Chile

00:45:11.059 --> 00:45:16.769
without having many licenses in the region. So and then

00:45:16.789 --> 00:45:20.449
obviously there are duties and customs. That's not our

00:45:20.469 --> 00:45:25.159
focus. Our focus is the e-commerce that happens in each one

00:45:25.179 --> 00:45:30.210
of the countries and we believe it's a huge market for that.

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:41.349
Hi. I wanted to know about how you started actually I mean

00:45:41.369 --> 00:45:44.119
you can find the technical founder and as you said before

00:45:44.119 --> 00:45:52.319
you need someone on your team that is technical. And do you

00:45:52.339 --> 00:45:57.269
develop a prototype or something like that to show?

00:45:57.289 --> 00:46:03.299
Yes, my cousin has a technical background and he started the

00:46:03.319 --> 00:46:06.889
company with me. That's a fun story. We bought an auction

00:46:06.909 --> 00:46:10.699
software from a company that was called 'OpenSite' it was

00:46:10.719 --> 00:46:14.690
eventually bought by a larger player, I can't remember who.

00:46:14.710 --> 00:46:20.139
They were in North Carolina and we bought it for 15,000

00:46:20.159 --> 00:46:23.980
dollars. It was a terrible technology but it enabled us to

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:27.349
launch, we knew we were going to have lots of competition

00:46:27.369 --> 00:46:31.949
and in the meantime we had our own developers doing our own

00:46:31.969 --> 00:46:40.619
platform. But initially we bought an off the shelf software,

00:46:40.639 --> 00:46:43.429
which we installed in the servers and we started with that.

00:46:43.449 --> 00:46:51.299
You mentioned one of your goals is present in all of Latin

00:46:51.319 --> 00:46:54.599
American countries. I was wondering why you have not

00:46:54.619 --> 00:47:00.750
penetrated the few counties we se in Central America?

00:47:00.769 --> 00:47:05.460
Partly it's because the technology that we have, that we are

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:13.589
changing, it's costly for us to do each country. Each new

00:47:13.609 --> 00:47:17.259
country is costly so typically to add one feature in Brazil

00:47:17.279 --> 00:47:21.460
has a better payoff than launching in Bolivia. But that's

00:47:21.480 --> 00:47:23.949
the reason why we are completely overhauling our technology

00:47:23.969 --> 00:47:26.739
so that we are able to launch in many more companies and

00:47:26.739 --> 00:47:35.940
move much more rapidly than we have been in the past.

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:42.269
I get the sense that you move really fast.

00:47:42.289 --> 00:47:50.049
[inaudible], I want to know if you were here today as a

00:47:50.069 --> 00:47:55.029
student and you got an idea, you need to run as fast again

00:47:55.049 --> 00:47:57.299
or will you wait? I mean it's case dependent...

00:47:57.299 --> 00:48:00.299
If you're going to be in the technology industry you need to

00:48:00.299 --> 00:48:03.529
run faster than you can. And that's why you need a great

00:48:03.529 --> 00:48:08.670
team and basically it's very stressful and it doesn't stop,

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:09.690
it's always like that.

00:48:09.690 --> 00:48:39.690
[Inaudible] if you setup the last ten years most of the

00:49:19.069 --> 00:49:21.579
largest countries in Latin America are

00:49:21.599 --> 00:49:26.789
[inaudible] how do you see the next ten years

00:49:26.809 --> 00:49:30.130
[inaudible] proven business models here and there or is it

00:49:30.150 --> 00:49:34.759
to invest in new business models?

00:49:34.779 --> 00:49:38.199
I don't know it's a hard question to answer but what I will

00:49:38.199 --> 00:49:41.799
say is it's very hard to replicate a business model in the

00:49:41.819 --> 00:49:44.759
region because you cannot replicate it you need to adapt it.

00:49:44.779 --> 00:49:53.170
There are many differences so that's what I would say.

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:54.190
[Inaudible]

00:49:54.190 --> 00:50:19.349
Well there are many. Obviously the group buying stuff is

00:50:19.369 --> 00:50:24.079
extremely hot and it's working very well over there as well.

00:50:24.099 --> 00:50:28.759
Games on the internet; we do some payments for some gaming

00:50:28.779 --> 00:50:32.940
companies in the region and they are growing very fast. The

00:50:32.960 --> 00:50:34.269
biggest platform is

00:50:34.289 --> 00:50:38.089
[inaudible] in Brazil but Facebook is growing very fast in

00:50:38.109 --> 00:50:44.500
the region and in Brazil. So I see people doing very well in

00:50:44.519 --> 00:50:45.549
those spaces and....

00:50:45.569 --> 00:50:46.569
[Inaudible]

00:50:46.569 --> 00:51:16.569
It's very tough to build it because you need to create scale

00:51:35.579 --> 00:51:39.559
and it's very difficult to reach scale. I think that its

00:51:39.579 --> 00:51:46.599
easier, well Check out now for Android for example and

00:51:46.619 --> 00:51:50.900
Facebook credits, well they don't use real money, they just

00:51:50.920 --> 00:52:09.119
use Facebook credit so in one way it's complimentary.

00:52:09.139 --> 00:52:10.139
[Inaudible]

00:52:10.139 --> 00:52:14.679
I don't know. I think there might be more than one but

00:52:14.699 --> 00:52:24.170
probably the largest will have a substantial share of the

00:52:24.190 --> 00:52:28.949
value created if you will. So probably there is going to be

00:52:28.969 --> 00:52:32.609
more than one. If you look at the credit cards industry,

00:52:32.629 --> 00:52:35.279
that's probably similar to what will happen online.

00:52:35.299 --> 00:52:40.849
Within the technology sector or maybe beyond the technology

00:52:40.869 --> 00:52:42.529
sector

00:52:42.549 --> 00:52:51.859
[Inaudible] what is your opinion about that?

00:52:51.879 --> 00:53:01.789
I see a lot more entrepreneurship in the region, endeavor

00:53:01.809 --> 00:53:05.829
this foundation that selected us in 1999 has been doing an

00:53:05.849 --> 00:53:09.500
amazing job in the region, through universities and working

00:53:09.519 --> 00:53:14.569
with governments and everyone is very aware of how valuable

00:53:14.589 --> 00:53:17.980
it is to have entrepreneurs in a country because they

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:25.549
generate mobile progress, mobility within a society and

00:53:25.569 --> 00:53:31.949
innovation and jobs and so there's a very big focus on

00:53:31.969 --> 00:53:38.349
entrepreneurship in other countries and I see many people

00:53:38.369 --> 00:53:42.329
doing stuff so I think the environment is very good. And

00:53:42.349 --> 00:53:45.679
there is venture capital, obviously it is not Silicon Valley

00:53:45.699 --> 00:53:49.079
but it's a big change from what it was eleven years ago.

00:53:49.099 --> 00:53:51.599
Talking about eleven years ago, I remember seeing the first

00:53:51.599 --> 00:53:54.099
advertisement for Mercado Libre and I don't think I even

00:53:54.099 --> 00:54:04.929
knew what online shopping was. And there really was a lot of

00:54:04.949 --> 00:54:12.629
players, what was the strategy to differentiate yourself and

00:54:12.649 --> 00:54:15.399
launch a brand when most people, even middle class had no

00:54:15.399 --> 00:54:16.649
idea what online shopping was?

00:54:16.649 --> 00:54:19.899
So I think what we wanted to do is do enough advertising to

00:54:19.899 --> 00:54:22.899
make sure we would be amongst the survivors but do as little

00:54:22.899 --> 00:54:25.899
as we could to save as much money as possible because we

00:54:25.899 --> 00:54:28.649
knew that we were just throwing money out the window because

00:54:28.649 --> 00:54:33.659
it was like saying, advertising something and then when

00:54:33.679 --> 00:54:38.319
people go to the supermarket it's not there. Internet

00:54:38.339 --> 00:54:42.839
penetration was 3% and TV, you target a 100% of the

00:54:42.859 --> 00:54:47.759
population so straight away you're wasting 97% of the money.

00:54:47.779 --> 00:54:53.400
We did very little TV but we did enough so that, because

00:54:53.420 --> 00:54:55.759
interestingly enough the venture capital, I know the

00:54:55.779 --> 00:55:00.349
financial community were the ones asking, pushing for people

00:55:00.369 --> 00:55:04.190
to spend money, so we were like ok we have to do something

00:55:04.210 --> 00:55:06.609
otherwise they won't invest in our round of financing

00:55:06.609 --> 00:55:09.210
because they won't think we will survive. But we know that

00:55:09.210 --> 00:55:15.079
this is not the way to go. I would definitely save that

00:55:15.099 --> 00:55:23.279
money. It was a waste of money.

00:55:23.299 --> 00:55:25.929
We have time for one more question if there is?

00:55:25.949 --> 00:55:26.949
Thank you.

